  "text": "We are given:\n\n$$\n\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\tan^{-1}(y) = \\tan^{-1}(xy)\n$$\n\nWe are to find the ratio $ \\frac{x}{y} $, where $ x, y > 0 $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the identity for tangent of sum of two angles\n\nWe recall the identity:\n\n$$\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}\n$$\n\nLet $ A = \\tan^{-1}(x) $, $ B = \\tan^{-1}(y) $, so:\n\n$$\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{x + y}{1 - xy}\n$$\n\nBut we are told:\n\n$$\n\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\tan^{-1}(y) = \\tan^{-1}(xy)\n$$\n\nThis implies:\n\n$$\n\\tan(A + B) = \\tan(\\tan^{-1}(xy)) = xy\n$$\n\nSo now we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x + y}{1 - xy} = xy\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Solve the equation\n\nMultiply both sides by $ 1 - xy $:\n\n$$\nx + y = xy(1 - xy)\n$$\n\n$$\nx + y = xy - x^2y^2\n$$\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n$$\nxy - x^2y^2 - x - y = 0\n$$\n\nFactor or simplify if possible. Let’s try factoring:\n\nLet’s rearrange:\n\n$$\nxy - x - y = x^2y^2\n$$\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\n$$\nxy - x - y + 1 = x^2y^2 + 1\n$$\n\nNow notice the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n(x - 1)(y - 1) = x^2y^2 + 1\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n(x - 1)(y - 1) = x^2y^2 + 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the ratio $ \\frac{x}{y} $\n\nLet’s denote $ r = \\frac{x}{y} $, so $ x = ry $. Substitute into the equation:\n\n$$\n(x - 1)(y - 1) = x^2y^2 + 1\n$$\n\n$$\n(ry - 1)(y - 1) = (ry)^2 y^2 + 1\n$$\n\n$$\n(ry - 1)(y - 1) = r^2 y^4 + 1\n$$\n\nExpand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n(ry - 1)(y - 1) = ry^2 - ry - y + 1 = ry^2 - y - ry + 1\n$$\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\nry^2 - y - ry + 1 = r^2 y^4 + 1\n$$\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\n$$\nry^2 - y - ry = r^2 y^4\n$$\n\n$$\nry^2 - ry - y = r^2 y^4\n$$\n\nFactor:\n\n$$\ny(r^2 y^2 - r - 1) = r^2 y^4\n$$\n\nDivide both sides by $ y $ (since $ y > 0 $):\n\n$$\nr^2 y^2 - r - 1 = r^2 y^3\n$$\n\nRearrange:\n\n$$\nr^2 y^3 - r^2 y^2 + r + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a cubic equation in terms of $ r $, but perhaps we can guess a solution.\n\nTry $ r = 1 $:\n\n$$\n1^2 y^3 - 1^2 y^2 + 1 + 1 = y^3 - y^2 + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nTry $ y = 1 $:\n\n$$\n1 - 1 + 2 = 2 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $ y = 2 $:\n\n$$\n8 - 4 + 2 = 6 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $ y = 1 $ again, but this doesn't work.\n\nLet’s try $ r = \\frac{x}{y} = 1 $, and test $ x = y $:\n\nLet $ x = y $, then:\n\n$$\n\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\tan^{-1}(x) = 2 \\tan^{-1}(x) = \\tan^{-1}(x^2)\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n2 \\tan^{-1}(x) = \\tan^{-1}(x^2)\n$$\n\nThis implies:\n\n$$\n2 \\tan^{-1}(x) = \\tan^{-1}(x^2)\n$$\n\nBut this is only possible if $ x = 1 $, since:\n\n$$\n\\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\quad \\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\quad \\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the only solution is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{x = y}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{x}{y} = 1}\n$$",
